WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.840
Can a protein read its own tail?

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:13.180
Src does. A fatty acid, myristate, anchors Src to the inner face of the plasma

3
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:21.600
membrane. Then come an SH3 domain, an SH2 domain, a linker, the kinase domain, and a

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.720
short tail. The tail holds tyrosine 527, six residues from its end.

5
00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:33.443
The enzyme Csk puts a phosphate on that tyrosine.

6
00:00:34.203 --> 00:00:35.663
The tail folds back.

7
00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:41.283
The protein's own SH2 domain grips the phosphotyrosine.

8
00:00:41.963 --> 00:00:45.023
Its own SH3 domain grips the linker.

9
00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:49.643
Both settle on the back of the kinase and hold it inactive.

10
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:52.797
The clamp is deliberately loose.

11
00:00:53.537 --> 00:00:58.757
The tail fits the SH2 pocket poorly, so a better ligand can pull it away.

12
00:00:59.557 --> 00:01:02.557
A phosphorylated PDGF receptor does this.

13
00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:07.077
So does the kinase FAK, once integrins have switched it on.

14
00:01:07.837 --> 00:01:12.817
A proline-rich partner such as beta-arrestin can take the SH3 domain.

15
00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:20.157
Once open, the kinase phosphorylates tyrosine 416 in its own activation loop, and

16
00:01:20.157 --> 00:01:21.157
stays active.

17
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:29.057
The viral protein, v-Src, has lost the tail, and tyrosine 527 with it.

18
00:01:29.697 --> 00:01:34.637
The SH2 domain has nothing to grip, so the clamp never forms.

19
00:01:35.597 --> 00:01:41.757
Changing tyrosine 527 to phenylalanine in c-Src does the same, and makes it

20
00:01:41.757 --> 00:01:48.177
transform cells. The viral form is always on because it has lost its off-switch.
